# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.venv/
venv/
env/

# Build / packaging artifacts (pyproject build, wheels)
# ROOT-anchored, exactly like /dist/ one line down and for the same reason: the unanchored form
# matches a directory named `build` ANYWHERE, and master-reference/build/ holds SOURCE (the
# sites-vite-plugin that vite.config.ts imports). Unanchored, that plugin was silently ignored,
# never committed, and the first CI run of the master-reference workflow failed with
#   vite.config.ts(3,23): error TS2307: Cannot find module './build/sites-vite-plugin.ts'
# while every LOCAL run passed, because the file sat in the worktree. Local-green/CI-red with no
# code difference is the signature of an ignore rule eating a tracked dependency.
/build/
/dist/
*.whl
_wheeltest/

# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
# Office owner-lock files leak last-editor identity, and their ~$ prefix defeats basename rules.
~$*

# Editors
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp

# Test caches / artifacts
.pytest_cache/
/.pytest_tmp_*/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.coverage

# Tool run outputs (never commit collected/generated network data)
*.log
migration_collection_*/
Migration_Assessment_AUTOFILLED_*
Migration_Diff_*.xlsx
topology.mmd
topology.dot

# ...and the same class by SHAPE, not by the one prefix we happened to use.
#
# The rules above are NAME-BOUND. The engine takes a free-choice `--output <name>.xlsx` — CLAUDE.md's
# own documented invocation — and writes ~12 siblings beside it (docmeta.CLI_ARTIFACT_SUFFIX: the
# workbook, explorer, runbook, design, mop, crd, engagement, archreview, opshandbook and deck, plus
# .snapshot.json / .run_manifest.json / .phase_timings.json). Measured before adding this block:
# AcmeBank_Q1.snapshot.json, AcmeBank_Q1_mop.docx, Assessment_20270101.xlsx and
# captures/sw1/show_running-config.txt were ALL committable. Only a run named
# `Migration_Assessment_AUTOFILLED_*` was covered.
#
# That matters most for the raw captures: a running-config carries SNMP communities, TACACS keys,
# enable secrets and VPN PSKs — exactly what `--redact-collection` exists to scrub. One `git add -A`
# is all it takes, and this repo has already had `git add -A` sweep in files nobody intended.
*.snapshot.json
*.run_manifest.json
*.phase_timings.json
*_runbook.docx
*_design.docx
*_mop.docx
*_crd.docx
*_engagement.docx
*_archreview.docx
*_ops_handbook.docx
*_deck.pptx
*_explorer.html
*_IP_CROSSWALK.xlsx
show_*.txt

# Every generated office/certificate surface is client-bearing regardless of
# basename. Explicit negations below are the only reviewable exceptions.
*.xlsx
*.docx
*.pptx
*.precert.json
*.precert-readiness.json

